        <h2>NIE vs TIE: What's the Difference?</h2>
        <p>Two essential documents often confuse applicants. Here's what you need to know.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="msv__two-col">
        <div class="msv__two-col-card">
          <div class="msv__two-col-icon">🔢</div>
          <h3>NIE Number</h3>
          <p><strong>NIE</strong> stands for Número de Identidad de Extranjero (foreigner's tax identification number). It's a unique identification number assigned to all foreigners in Spain for tax, banking, and employment purposes.</p>
          <p><strong>Who needs it:</strong> Anyone opening a bank account, working, starting a business, or renting property in Spain.</p>
          <p><strong>When you get it:</strong> You can apply for a NIE before obtaining residency. Many residency applicants get their NIE first.</p>
          <p><a href="/nie-number-spain/">Learn more about NIE →</a></p>
        </div>
        <div class="msv__two-col-card">
          <div class="msv__two-col-icon">🪪</div>
          <h3>TIE Card</h3>
          <p><strong>TIE</strong> stands for Tarjeta de Identidad de Extranjero (foreigner's identity card). It's your official residency card, issued after visa approval, proving your legal residency status.</p>
          <p><strong>Who needs it:</strong> Anyone granted a residency visa (Non-Lucrative, Digital Nomad, Student, Work, etc.).</p>
          <p><strong>When you get it:</strong> After traveling to Spain with visa approval, you collect your TIE card from the nearest police station or National Police office.</p>
          <p><a href="/tie-card-spain/">Learn more about TIE →</a></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
